from lesson_writer import author,CPP

def testlink(l):return 'Read the actual '+l(CPP/'support/ObjectOracle.java','Java object oracle')+', '+l(CPP/'support/objects.cpp','native harness')+' and '+l(CPP/'tools/object_differential.py','fixture driver')+'. The inspected full-object report has 9,244 matching observations with zero failures. That is a corpus total; generic ERROR matching does not prove exact failure types.'
def w(name,purpose,steps,adapt,detail,exercise,limits):
 author('src/org/minima/objects/mmr/'+name+'.md',purpose,steps,adapt,lambda l:detail+'\n\n'+testlink(l),exercise,limits+' The source-map status remains partial and full parity not_verified.')
w('MMREntryNumber','An unbounded decimal position value used for MMR tree arithmetic. Unlike MiniNumber, it does not impose the monetary range or 64-digit precision limit.',[
 'Store a BigDecimal with unlimited math context. Constructors permit large values and decimal scales; the type itself does not enforce an integer or nonnegative position invariant.',
 'increment/decrement add or subtract one, mult2 doubles the value, div2 halves it exactly, and floor rounds toward negative infinity. For an odd integer, halving creates a fractional value before a caller applies floor.',
 'modulo uses BigDecimal remainder semantics. Numeric comparison ignores representational scale; toString strips trailing zeros and prints plain decimal text.',
 'Write the decimal scale as a complete MiniNumber, then wrap the signed unscaled BigInteger bytes in MiniData. This differs from MiniNumber’s compact one-byte scale/length representation.',
 'Read scale with MiniNumber.getAsInt, read the unscaled byte array and reconstruct an unlimited BigDecimal. Empty unscaled bytes fail BigInteger construction.'
], 'C++ reuses the BigInt building block but stores an independent int32 scale and does not call MiniNumber limit checks on the position. Halving an odd unscaled integer multiplies by five and increments scale; doubling and scale alignment remain exact. Additional native dividePositive/multiplyDecimal32 helpers implement rounded chain-weight arithmetic and are adapters beyond this Java file’s API.',
 'The entry branch tests unscaled values including negatives, 2^128 and 2^256+1 at scales -10, -1, 0, 1, 2 and 15. It compares wire bytes and derived arithmetic. The mmrentry branch connects positions to parent/sibling/child calculations. Arbitrarily huge allocations and every signed scale boundary remain outside this finite corpus.',
 'For entry number -3, what are div2 and floor(div2)? Why would ordinary integer division toward zero give the wrong parent position?',
 'Exact BigDecimal-returning APIs are adapted rather than exposed literally. Empty BigInteger and scale-overflow errors use native exception categories; generic comparison failures do not establish those categories.')
w('MMR','A Merkle Mountain Range stores a growing forest of perfect binary trees. Each node commits to both a hash and a sum; block-specific layers share older entries through parent links.',[
 'Index local entries by the string row:entryNumber. Track the largest entry in each of 256 rows and the next leaf number. A lookup searches the current layer and its parents until a requested oldest block time, optionally falling back once to a configured MegaMMR. Missing data is represented by an empty entry object.',
 'A child MMR finalizes its parent if needed, advances block time by one and copies the parent’s peaks into its own local layer. A peak at row r represents 2^r leaves, so summing those powers reconstructs the next leaf number.',
 'Appending puts a new leaf at row zero and increments the next index. While that entry is a right child, combine it with its left sibling to create a parent one row higher. Stop at a left child: it is the new peak. This is analogous to carrying through trailing one bits during binary increment.',
 'Peaks are the maximum left-child entries in each row, visited from highest row downward. To form one root, repeatedly insert peak hash-and-sum values into a temporary MMR until only one peak remains. These values are reused directly; they are not rehashed as ordinary object leaves.',
 'finalizeSet computes and caches peaks and root, then marks the set finalized. setEntry refuses writes when finalized. This is a procedural guard, not deep immutability: exposed entry/data references can still matter.',
 'A proof to a peak records successive siblings until no sibling exists. A full proof appends the peak-to-root path created by repeatedly building MMRs from the peaks. checkProof accepts a result equal to either the full root or one of the peaks.',
 'Time-aware validation first finds the parent layer matching the proof time and checks the proof there. It then looks for the leaf at or after that time; an empty result or identical data is accepted, while a newer differing leaf invalidates the old proof.',
 'updateEntry assumes the old proof was already validated. It records a new leaf, rebuilds ancestors toward the current peaks, and obtains each sibling from sufficiently recent layers or, when absent, from the corresponding old proof chunk. It stores those siblings locally as it proceeds.',
 'pruneTree recursively removes children below a zero-sum node while retaining their parent commitment. scanUnspendableTree additionally propagates unspendable metadata, prunes children that are unspendable or zero, and records removed row-zero entry numbers. The metadata is not serialized by MMRData.',
 'Wire output contains block time, next leaf number, local entry count and local entries in Hashtable enumeration order. Parent links and cached metadata are omitted. Reading replaces the local table/maxima, loads entries through setEntry and finalizes. deepCopy is this serialization round trip, so it is not a clone of the complete parent graph.',
 'The pinned reader does not reset the existing finalized flag before calling setEntry. Reading into a previously finalized receiver can therefore reject incoming entries while consuming them; fresh-object round trips do not reveal that situation.'
], 'Native entries and MMRData use shared_ptr to preserve meaningful sharing between layers. JavaHashtable reproduces Java string hashing, bucket growth and enumeration order because that order reaches JSON and serialized bytes. The MegaMMR dependency is supplied through a callback. Native getAllEntries returns a vector snapshot of shared entries rather than Java’s mutable Hashtable, so direct container mutation is not equivalent.',
 'The object corpus builds sizes 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 15, 16, 17, 31, 32 and 33 in normal, prune, scan and child modes. Leaves vary zero sums and unspendable flags. Normal mode gets and validates every leaf proof. Child mode adds a leaf, checks a historical proof, replaces its original leaf with zero data and checks that proof again. It compares local wire/JSON, roots and reported proof results, followed by Java-produced read/truncation/trailing cases. These small trees exercise peak transitions; they do not prove all memory, ownership or live MegaMMR behavior.',
 'Draw an MMR with five leaves: which peak rows exist? Add a sixth and identify the new hashes. Then explain how a valid proof from an older block can become invalid after its leaf is updated.',
 'The lesson follows upstream-study’s pinned source. The workspace’s separate src copy adds a heap-watermark check in readDataStream; that fork-only addition is not treated as an obligation of this pinned port. Native logging is missing in several paths, deepCopy propagates errors instead of Java’s logged null, and invalid rows are rejected before some Java partial mutations. Parent/cache aliasing and finalized-receiver reads need focused review beyond the ordinary object fixture.')
